<div class="definition-example provenance-definition" role="group" aria-labelledby="{{DEFINITION_PREFIX}}-title">
<div class="definition-preview">
<div class="definition-preview-top"><h3 id="{{DEFINITION_PREFIX}}-title">Provenance</h3></div>
<div class="definition-meaning"><div class="definition-hero"><figure class="definition-illustration"><img src="assets/definition-provenance-v1.png" alt="Two source documents connected through a comparison to a result document, with a magnifier for inspection." width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Sources and operations remain traceable.</figcaption></figure><div class="definition-core"><p>Provenance is the declared origin and transformation chain for an exact object version. It identifies the sources or earlier objects on which that version depends, the typed relationships and operations connecting them, the owners or processes involved and the authority standing at each relevant step.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="definition-formal"><code>Prov(x) = [(sourceᵢ, relationᵢ, operationᵢ, ownerᵢ, postureᵢ)]*</code></div></div>
<details class="lens-record-detail definition-record-detail definition-structure-detail"><summary>Read the full definition</summary>
<div class="definition-symbols"><p class="record-label">Symbols in the formula</p><dl class="definition-notation"><div><dt><var>x</var></dt><dd>The exact version of the record, claim, rule or other object whose origins are being examined.</dd></div><div><dt><code>sourceᵢ</code></dt><dd>An identifiable source or earlier object version used at step <var>i</var>.</dd></div><div><dt><code>relationᵢ</code></dt><dd>The declared relationship at that step, under the applicable connection rules.</dd></div><div><dt><code>operationᵢ</code></dt><dd>What was done with that source: for example, a comparison, adaptation or generated projection.</dd></div><div><dt><code>ownerᵢ</code></dt><dd>The owner or process responsible for that step.</dd></div><div><dt><code>postureᵢ</code></dt><dd>The authority standing at that step, such as a recorded source or an accepted definition.</dd></div><div><dt><code>[…]*</code></dt><dd>A sequence of trail entries. Any missing segment must be recorded explicitly, with an owner responsible for its review.</dd></div></dl></div>
<p class="record-label definition-scope-label">Scope and boundaries</p><dl class="definition-fields"><div><dt>Applicability</dt><dd>Tracing the origins and transformations of definitions, records, claims, decisions and results before relying on, adapting or publishing them.</dd></div><div><dt>Exclusions</dt><dd>Establishing truth, granting permission, validating a result or accepting a record. Those require their own evidence and authority routes.</dd></div><div><dt>Semantic owner and scope</dt><dd>The definition owner is responsible for the meaning of provenance in the Lab’s lineage scope. A service owner maintains the particular export trail within its own responsibility.</dd></div><div><dt>Boundary conditions</dt><dd>Sources, versions, relationship types and operations must be identifiable. Missing or uncertain steps remain visible and have an owner route for review.</dd></div></dl>
<div class="definition-voices"><dl><div><dt>Plain-language voice</dt><dd>The trail that lets someone inspect where a record came from and what happened to it.</dd></div><div><dt>Domain-context voice</dt><dd>For the {{CASE_ACCOUNT}} incident assessment, the trail identifies snapshot {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}, output {{CASE_OUTPUT}}, software revision {{CASE_SERVICE_REVISION}}, comparison method version {{CASE_METHOD_VERSION}} and investigation session {{CASE_SESSION}}. It also records their roles and authority standing.</dd></div></dl></div>
<div class="definition-review-fields"><dl class="definition-fields"><div><dt>Identity and version</dt><dd>This article illustrates the Lab’s selected Provenance definition. The reference library preserves its exact identity and version.</dd></div><div><dt>Sources and standing</dt><dd>The meaning and trail expression come from the Lab’s Provenance definition. This presentation explains that meaning; the business records below are invented examples.</dd></div><div><dt>Primary consumers</dt><dd>Reviewers, source imports, measurement records and agents examining the basis of a conclusion.</dd></div><div><dt>Testable conditions</dt><dd>A reviewer can locate the declared sources and operations, distinguish their roles and identify any unresolved segment and its responsible owner.</dd></div><div><dt>Registry and index</dt><dd>Selected authority is looked up through the definition registry and its synchronized index. This article adds no definition selection.</dd></div><div><dt>Drift and challenge route</dt><dd>Changes to the meaning go to the definition owner. Changes or gaps in a particular trail go to the owner responsible for that record and its source review.</dd></div></dl></div><p class="definition-image-credit">Illustration generated with imagegen. <a href="research/definition-card-images.json">Prompt and asset record</a>.</p></details>
</div>
</div>
